---
import DeepDiveLayout from "../../components/redesign/DeepDiveLayout.astro";
import data from "../../../public/data/surveillance_wash_latest.json";
import t2 from "../../../public/data/surveillance_wash_tier2_latest.json";

const h = data.headline;
const t2pop = t2.population;
const t2thresh = t2.thresholds;
const strict = t2thresh.find((x: any) => x.name === "strict");

function fmtPct(x: number, dp = 4): string { return (x * 100).toFixed(dp) + "%"; }
function fmtUsd(x: number): string {
  if (x >= 1e9) return `$${(x/1e9).toFixed(2)}B`;
  if (x >= 1e6) return `$${(x/1e6).toFixed(1)}M`;
  if (x >= 1e3) return `$${(x/1e3).toFixed(0)}K`;
  return `$${x.toFixed(0)}`;
}
function fmtNum(x: number): string { return x.toLocaleString(); }

// Static lookup of market questions for the top Tier 1 affected markets.
// These IDs are stable until the next full Tier 1 rerun; questions resolved
// via the Gamma API once and frozen here. New IDs after a rerun would fall
// back to the raw ID display.
const MARKET_QUESTIONS: Record<string, string> = {
  "240380": "Will Donald J. Trump win the 2024 Republican presidential nomination?",
  "249497": "Will the Fed increase interest rates by 0 bps after its May meeting?",
  "248974": "Will 'Creed III' gross more than $35 million domestically on its opening weekend?",
  "250113": "Will U.S. inflation be greater than 0.1% from March to April 2023?",
  "247266": "Will Donald J. Trump be indicted by March 31, 2023?",
  "248578": "Will the price of $ETH be above $1700 on February 10?",
  "248975": "Will 'Creed III' gross more than $40 million domestically on its opening weekend?",
  "248890": "Will Arbitrum airdrop happen by March 31st?",
  "248820": "Will Andrew Tate be released by April 30?",
  "249524": "2023 FIDE World Chess Championship: Ian Nepomniachtchi vs. Ding Liren",
};

// Top-wallets summary stats (no wallet addresses shown).
const tw = t2.top_wallets_strict as any[];
const tw_avg_trips = tw.reduce((s, w) => s + w.total_round_trips, 0) / tw.length;
const tw_avg_tokens = tw.reduce((s, w) => s + w.n_tokens_flagged, 0) / tw.length;
const tw_avg_vol = tw.reduce((s, w) => s + w.total_round_trip_vol, 0) / tw.length;
const tw_avg_churn = tw.reduce((s, w) => s + w.avg_churn, 0) / tw.length;
const tw_max_trips = Math.max(...tw.map(w => w.total_round_trips));
const tw_max_tokens = Math.max(...tw.map(w => w.n_tokens_flagged));

const lede = `Two complementary tests on the full ${h.n_trades_total.toLocaleString()}-trade panel. Tier 1 (self-matched trades, where maker and taker are the same wallet) finds ${h.n_self_matched.toLocaleString()} matches (${fmtPct(h.self_matched_share_by_count, 4)} of trades, ${fmtPct(h.self_matched_share_by_volume, 4)} of dollar volume), all confined to the 2022-2023 launch window. Tier 2 (round-trip wash, single wallet buying and selling the same token through different counterparties) flags ${fmtNum(strict.n_pairs)} (wallet, token) pairs at the strict threshold across ${fmtNum(strict.n_wallets)} wallets totaling ${fmtUsd(strict.round_trip_volume)} of round-trip volume. The Tier 2 strict-flagged set is dominated by algorithmic market makers operating across hundreds of tokens; it represents an upper bound on wash-trading activity, with the true subset bounded below by Tier 1's near-zero floor. Tier 3 (linked-wallet clusters) is required to isolate wash trading from inventory rotation.`;
---
<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Wash Trading (Tier 1)"
  description="Trades where maker and taker are the same wallet."
  eyebrow="DV-PMI Surveillance · Wash trading"
  lede={lede}
  asOf={data.as_of ? `Cumulative through week ${data.as_of}` : undefined}
  citeKey="dellavedova2026wash"
  citeTitle="Wash Trading Index (Tier 1)"
  citePath="surveillance/wash-trading">

  <Fragment slot="chart">
    <div style="padding:24px; background: var(--c-surface, #0c1622); border: 1px solid var(--c-border, #1c2a3a); border-radius: 6px;">
      <table style="width:100%;">
        <tr><th></th><th style="text-align:right;">Share by count</th><th style="text-align:right;">Share by volume</th></tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Self-matched (Tier 1)</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;font-family:monospace;">{fmtPct(h.self_matched_share_by_count, 4)}</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;font-family:monospace;">{fmtPct(h.self_matched_share_by_volume, 4)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>All other trades</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;font-family:monospace;">{fmtPct(1 - h.self_matched_share_by_count, 4)}</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;font-family:monospace;">{fmtPct(1 - h.self_matched_share_by_volume, 4)}</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The denominator is the full on-chain panel: every resolved Polymarket match from launch (Nov 2022) to the most recent refresh. The self-matched fraction is the share of matches where the maker and taker address are equal.
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Top affected markets</h2>
  <p>
    Markets ranked by number of self-matched trades. Most are early bootstrap markets where the venue
    operator or a deployment-test wallet seeded liquidity by trading against itself before public launch.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Market</th><th>Market ID</th><th>Self-matched trades</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
    {data.top_markets.map(m => (
      <tr>
        <td style="max-width: 480px;">{MARKET_QUESTIONS[m.market_id] ?? <em>resolved market #{m.market_id}</em>}</td>
        <td class="mono">{m.market_id}</td>
        <td class="mono">{m.n.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(m.vol)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h2>By year</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Self-matched trades</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
    {data.by_year.map(y => (
      <tr>
        <td class="mono">{y.year}</td>
        <td class="mono">{y.n.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(y.vol)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h2>Methodology — Tier 1</h2>
  <p>{data.tier1_methodology}</p>
  <p>
    The test is implemented as a single pass through the full processed-trades file via duckdb:
    {`{`} maker_address.lower() == taker_address.lower() {`}`}. Pre-filter to the small self-matched
    subset, then aggregate counts, volumes, top markets, top wallets, and weekly time series. No
    sampling; the denominator is the entire panel.
  </p>

  <hr style="margin: 48px 0; border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border, #1c2a3a);" />

  <h2>Tier 2 — Round-trip wash</h2>
  <p>
    Tier 2 catches a wallet that buys and sells the same outcome token through different counterparties,
    netting to zero or near-zero exposure while inflating volume. For each <code>(wallet, token_id)</code>
    pair we compute the churn ratio: <code>2 × min(buy_vol, sell_vol) / (buy_vol + sell_vol)</code>,
    ranging from 0 (one-sided position) to 1 (perfectly balanced two-sided activity). A pair is flagged
    at a given threshold if churn_ratio, total volume, and minimum-round-trips all clear the bar.
  </p>

  <p>
    Population denominators: <strong>{fmtNum(t2pop.n_two_sided_pairs)}</strong> two-sided (wallet, token)
    pairs in the panel, across <strong>{fmtNum(t2pop.n_wallets_with_two_sided_activity)}</strong> wallets,
    totaling <strong>{fmtUsd(t2pop.total_volume_in_two_sided_pairs)}</strong> of volume.
  </p>

  <h3>Threshold table</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Threshold</th>
      <th>Min churn</th>
      <th>Min volume</th>
      <th>Min round-trips</th>
      <th>Pairs flagged</th>
      <th>Wallets</th>
      <th>Round-trip volume</th>
    </tr>
    {t2thresh.map((row: any) => (
      <tr>
        <td><strong>{row.name}</strong></td>
        <td class="mono">{row.min_churn.toFixed(2)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(row.min_vol)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{row.min_trips}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtNum(row.n_pairs)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtNum(row.n_wallets)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(row.round_trip_volume)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h3>Churn-ratio distribution across all two-sided pairs</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Churn-ratio bin</th><th>Pairs</th><th>Share of pairs</th><th>Total volume</th></tr>
    {t2.churn_histogram.map((b: any) => (
      <tr>
        <td class="mono">[{b.bin_low.toFixed(1)}, {(b.bin_low + 0.1).toFixed(1)})</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtNum(b.n_pairs)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{((b.n_pairs / t2pop.n_two_sided_pairs) * 100).toFixed(1)}%</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(b.total_vol)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p>
    The distribution is bimodal. Many pairs cluster near zero (one-side-dominant positions: a wallet
    that mostly bought and then sold a small fraction, or vice versa), and a large mass clusters at
    high churn (0.8 and above). The high-churn mass is what Tier 2 thresholds isolate.
  </p>

  <h3>Trade activity of strict-threshold-flagged wallets, by year</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Trades by flagged wallets</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
    {t2.by_year_strict_flagged_wallets.map((y: any) => (
      <tr>
        <td class="mono">{y.year}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtNum(y.n_trades_by_flagged)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(y.vol_by_flagged)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p>
    Unlike Tier 1, where activity was confined to the 2022-2023 launch window, Tier 2 strict-threshold
    flagged wallets are highly active in 2024-2026, with trade counts and volumes growing each year.
    This is the dominant feature of the result and matches the broader Paper 1 finding that algorithmic
    wallets have grown to ~90 percent of Polymarket participation over this period.
  </p>

  <h3>Top-wallet profile (strict threshold; addresses not shown)</h3>
  <p>
    At the strict threshold, summary statistics over the top 20 wallets by round-trip volume:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Average</th><th>Maximum</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Tokens flagged per wallet</td>
      <td class="mono">{tw_avg_tokens.toFixed(0)}</td>
      <td class="mono">{fmtNum(tw_max_tokens)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Round-trips per wallet</td>
      <td class="mono">{fmtNum(Math.round(tw_avg_trips))}</td>
      <td class="mono">{fmtNum(tw_max_trips)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Round-trip volume per wallet</td>
      <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(tw_avg_vol)}</td>
      <td class="mono">{fmtUsd(Math.max(...tw.map((w: any) => w.total_round_trip_vol)))}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Average churn ratio</td>
      <td class="mono">{tw_avg_churn.toFixed(3)}</td>
      <td class="mono">{Math.max(...tw.map((w: any) => w.avg_churn)).toFixed(3)}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>What Tier 2 actually catches: algorithmic market making</h2>
  <p>
    The top-wallet profile makes the limitation of Tier 2 explicit. The top wallet has
    <strong>{fmtNum(tw_max_tokens)} tokens flagged</strong> at the strict threshold and the maximum
    round-trip count across the top 20 is <strong>{fmtNum(tw_max_trips)}</strong>. A wash trader
    making 5 or 10 manipulative round-trips would not generate {fmtNum(tw_max_trips)} balanced
    buy/sell cycles across hundreds of distinct outcome tokens. That activity profile is the
    signature of an algorithmic market maker rotating inventory across many markets simultaneously,
    not a manipulator inflating volume in one or two contracts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tier 2 as implemented therefore measures the <em>magnitude of algorithmic two-sided liquidity
    provision on Polymarket</em>, with wash trading as a subset embedded inside it. To isolate the
    wash-trading subset specifically, the screen needs counterparty-network structure: paired wallets
    trading repeatedly against each other in tight time windows with offsetting net exposure. That is
    Tier 3 (cluster wash with linked counterparties), forthcoming.
  </p>
  <p>
    We report Tier 2 here transparently rather than withhold it. The honest framing is "patterns
    consistent with high-volume balanced two-sided activity" — a population that contains both
    manipulative round-trip washing and legitimate algorithmic market making, with the latter
    quantitatively dominant. The strict-threshold number ({fmtNum(strict.n_wallets)} wallets,
    {fmtUsd(strict.round_trip_volume)} of round-trip volume) is the upper bound; the lower bound is
    near-zero from Tier 1.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 2 limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    {t2.interpretation_caveats.map((c: string) => <li>{c}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Tiers forthcoming</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tier 2b — Within-window round-trips.</strong> Add a temporal-window constraint (e.g. all round-trips within a 24-hour window) to remove long-horizon position rotation by market makers from the count. Sharpens Tier 2 toward wash-specific activity.</li>
    <li><strong>Tier 3 — Cluster wash (linked counterparties).</strong> Wallet pairs trading repeatedly against each other with offsetting net exposure. Requires counterparty-network construction across the full trade history. This is the only tier that can credibly isolate wash trading from inventory rotation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Polymarket's order-matching engine does not permit a wallet to fill its own resting order under
    normal operation; the residual self-matched count includes pre-launch test transactions,
    contract-deployment artifacts, and edge cases where the engine accepted a self-cross during a
    contract migration or upgrade. The near-zero share is the expected pattern for a competently-built
    venue and is a baseline against which the more permissive Tier 2 and Tier 3 results will be reported.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reporting the null finding explicitly is the point. A surveillance dashboard that only reported
    positive flags would not be a surveillance dashboard. The economic question "is the volume number
    inflated by self-matching" has been tested on the full on-chain record and the answer is no.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>
    A wallet can avoid Tier 1 detection by routing one side of the trade through a second address it
    controls. Tier 3 (cluster wash) is designed to catch this case and is forthcoming. The clustering
    threshold matters: legitimate algorithmic liquidity providers that operate paired makers/takers
    will generate false positives, so Tier 3 will be reported as "patterns consistent with" rather than
    "wash trades."
  </p>

  <h2>Source paper</h2>
  <p>
    Tier 1 is the simplest of the three-tier framework introduced in the DV-PMI surveillance methodology
    paper (forthcoming). The economic and regulatory analog is FINRA Rule 6140.
  </p>

  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    Tier 1: <a href="/data/surveillance_wash_latest.json">surveillance_wash_latest.json</a> &middot;
    <a href="/data/surveillance_wash_t1_weekly.csv">surveillance_wash_t1_weekly.csv</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Tier 2: <a href="/data/surveillance_wash_tier2_latest.json">surveillance_wash_tier2_latest.json</a> &middot;
    <a href="/data/surveillance_wash_tier2_top_wallets.csv">surveillance_wash_tier2_top_wallets.csv</a>
    (per-wallet detail; usage notice on <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a>)
  </p>

  <h2>Other surveillance indices</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/surveillance">&larr; All surveillance indices</a> &middot;
    <a href="/pii">Informed Trading (PII)</a> &middot;
    <a href="/surveillance/insider-timing">Insider Timing</a> &middot;
    <a href="/surveillance/adverse-selection">Adverse Selection</a> &middot;
    <a href="/surveillance/resolution-surprise">Resolution Surprise</a>
  </p>
</DeepDiveLayout>
